  "question": "How does the smallest-grammar size of the Rule 30 center-column prefix grow with n, relative to a matched random control?",
  "method": {
    "algorithm": "Re-Pair, then the residual start sequence binarised into a left-deep chain so the object is a CNF SLP",
    "upper_bound_only": "Computing the exactly smallest grammar is NP-hard. Every g(n) here is what Re-Pair found, an UPPER bound on g*(n). The comparison against the control is between two upper bounds from the same deterministic algorithm.",
    "size_measure": "number of CNF rules (2 symbols per RHS)",
    "derivation_checked": "every grammar is expanded and compared against its input"
  },
  "counting_null": {
    "formula": "|M(g)| = ((g+1)!)^2, so log2|M(g)| = 2*log2((g+1)!)",
    "meaning": "g_null(n) is the smallest rule count whose class is large enough to express an arbitrary n-bit string at all. A measured g(n) at or below g_null(n) would be uninformative by counting.",
    "reference": "docs/theory/finite-prefix-counting-bound.md",
    "note": "g_null is a lower bound on the exact g*(n) for almost all strings; Re-Pair gives an upper bound. The true curve lies between them."
  },
